dealers that would set up

00:10:40.270 --> 00:10:42.957
and they'd have half a cake table of cards

00:10:42.957 --> 00:10:45.110
and then half a table of comics and toys.

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:47.210
So there was definitely
a shift in the market

00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:49.210
and Gary saw it and

00:10:50.350 --> 00:10:53.520
despite his real love for baseball cards,

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:57.380
he was very ready to pivot to
wherever the market was going.

00:10:57.380 --> 00:10:59.460
And at that point, the
market was going to toys.

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:00.550
And so,

00:11:00.550 --> 00:11:03.670
I remember the very last
card show I ever did,

00:11:03.670 --> 00:11:05.250
I was already kind of burnt out.

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:06.653
I was already kind of done.

00:11:08.349 --> 00:11:09.410
I was spending a lot of money,

00:11:09.410 --> 00:11:11.160
not getting any in return,

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:13.840
the card, the value was
worthless it seemed.

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:15.833
And we did this last show,

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:18.203
which was in Hillsborough,

00:11:19.150 --> 00:11:20.860
Hillsborough, New Jersey.

00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:24.560
And I went to the show and I
walked in and Gary was there

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:27.310
and his entire table was full of toys,

00:11:27.310 --> 00:11:29.622
no cards at all, just all toys.

00:11:29.622 --> 00:11:32.050
- I didn't tell Brandon
I have his strategy.

00:11:32.050 --> 00:11:33.650
See he's setting up his cards,

00:11:33.650 --> 00:11:35.797
I start opening up these big boxes,

00:11:35.797 --> 00:11:40.203
and some wizard of oz and cat woman toys.

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:43.677
We're juniors in high school.

00:11:44.674 --> 00:11:47.963
Brandon looks over, he goes, I'm done.

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:52.750
- And I remember walking up
and I looked at him and I said,

00:11:52.750 --> 00:11:53.583
what is this?

00:11:53.583 --> 00:11:57.060
And I was kind of upset
because deep down I knew

00:11:57.060 --> 00:11:57.893
that was it.

00:11:57.893 --> 00:11:58.726
It was over.

00:11:58.726 --> 00:12:00.780
Like the baseball card
between us, we're done.

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:02.230
The whole industry was dying.

00:12:03.078 --> 00:12:03.911
And he goes,

00:12:03.911 --> 00:12:05.890
we're going to sell a ton of toys today.

00:12:05.890 --> 00:12:08.290
And I said, not me and I left.

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:10.880
I just, I was not, I couldn't do it.

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:12.280
- I know this is the future.

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:15.293
I literally was like...

00:12:15.293 --> 00:12:16.783
and I'm the oldest.

00:12:16.783 --> 00:12:17.800
Like in...

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:20.110
I fucking nailed it.

00:12:20.110 --> 00:12:25.110
As a set, nobody thought about
Marvel movies and star Wars.

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:27.230
I fucking nailed it.

00:12:27.230 --> 00:12:28.580
- I just, I couldn't,

00:12:28.580 --> 00:12:32.140
I couldn't change my mind in that moment,

00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:33.323
but Gary sure did.

00:12:34.526 --> 00:12:36.700
And the rest is history.

00:12:36.700 --> 00:12:38.270
They went out and he did toys,

00:12:38.270 --> 00:12:41.421
which is where the market
was for quite a while.

00:12:41.421 --> 00:12:45.170
Only now with the last three
years that you guys will know

00:12:45.170 --> 00:12:47.180
to come full circle back into cards,

00:12:47.180 --> 00:12:50.200
where now it seems cards
are the hottest thing going.

00:12:50.200 --> 00:12:51.590
I mean, it's just, it's unbelievable.

00:12:51.590 --> 00:12:54.270
So that was from cards,

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:55.880
all in on cards,

00:12:55.880 --> 00:13:00.010
then all in on toys and
then all back in on cards.

00:13:00.010 --> 00:13:01.760
- I believe we're on the,

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:04.900
we're in the second
quarter of four quarters

00:13:04.900 --> 00:13:08.030
of the sports card industry
exploding again and culture.

00:13:08.030 --> 00:13:09.670
And I think there's a ton of money

00:13:09.670 --> 00:13:11.340
and a lot of fun to be made

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:13.840
for a lot of entrepreneurs, kids,

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:15.560
especially sneaker kids

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:18.350
who can't get enough inventory
of hard to get sneakers.

00:13:18.350 --> 00:13:21.130
But if they want to go
buy 5,000 Giannis rookies,

00:13:21.130 --> 00:13:24.280
or 400 Darko rookies, they can.

00:13:24.280 --> 00:13:27.290
And so I'm excited because
it's something I love,

00:13:27.290 --> 00:13:30.490
something I understand, but
I'm in a new era in my life.

00:13:30.490 --> 00:13:32.390
It's not about the million dollars

00:13:32.390 --> 00:13:34.657
that I'll make in flipping
cards in the next three years,

00:13:34.657 --> 00:13:35.890
and I will,

00:13:35.890 --> 00:13:36.723
it's about

00:13:38.820 --> 00:13:43.160
the tens of thousands of
kids that I'm going to impact

00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:46.550
who are going to make a hundred
thousand bucks in profit

00:13:46.550 --> 00:13:47.640
in the next two years,

00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:51.140
instead of raising a million
dollars and losing it.

00:13:51.140 --> 00:13:52.740
And that I'm excited about.

00:13:52.740 --> 00:13:55.210
I'm excited about pushing
people and teaching them

00:13:55.210 --> 00:13:58.200
how to make money
instead of raising money.

00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:01.250
And raising money has become
the sport of entrepreneurship

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:03.150
in this era, not making it.

00:14:03.150 --> 00:14:05.640
And it's something that I'm
trying to bring conversation to.

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:08.270
- [Katie] So are you
guys flipping cards too?

00:14:08.270 --> 00:14:09.160
Throughout his career,

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:12.430
Gary often made pivots, but
then he started VaynerMedia,

00:14:12.430 --> 00:14:14.410
which then led him to go all in

00:14:14.410 --> 00:14:16.689
on his personal brand in 2014.

00:14:16.689 --> 00:14:17.681
My name is Staphon.

00:14:17.681 --> 00:14:22.681
I've been at VaynerMedia since
November, 2014, I believe.

00:14:22.700 --> 00:14:24.713
And during that time,

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:28.260
I've started out on Gary's team,

00:14:28.260 --> 00:14:31.090
working on team Gary with Drock
when it was just me and him

00:14:31.090 --> 00:14:32.240
pumping out videos.

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:35.210
And I've since then
transitioned to Vayner talent,

00:14:35.210 --> 00:14:36.740
which is basically what
I was doing for Gary,

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:38.340
what the team was doing for Gary,

00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:40.700
we do for other people who want

00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:41.910
to build their personal brand.

00:14:41.910 --> 00:14:42.930
- By the way, Staphon...

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:44.010
Let's show Staphon.

00:14:47.410 --> 00:14:49.243
Trying to get some ladies out of this.

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:50.993
Why not?

00:14:50.993 --> 00:14:53.390
This is so ridiculous.

00:14:53.390 --> 00:14:55.240
This is so ridiculous.

00:14:55.240 --> 00:14:58.026
Get down here Staphon, I
want you to work with me.

00:14:58.026 --> 00:14:58.859
- I mean,

00:14:58.859 --> 00:15:00.800
I think one of the things
that you learned early on

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:03.140
being on Gary's team is
to not be comfortable

00:15:03.140 --> 00:15:04.720
doing the same thing all the time.

00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:07.850
That like starting out,
there was "AskGaryVee"

00:15:07.850 --> 00:15:09.260
then it moved to DailyVee,

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:10.093
then it was like,

00:15:10.093 --> 00:15:12.550
we just want a bunch of
clips just to put out clips.

00:15:12.550 --> 00:15:13.530
And then it was just like,

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:16.820
just kind of always having
to think about what was next.

00:15:16.820 --> 00:15:17.653
I mean,

00:15:17.653 --> 00:15:19.450
the good thing with Gary is
that his mind was always there.

00:15:19.450 --> 00:15:21.240
So you would just kinda like, okay, cool.

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:22.620
And then you try to get ahead of it

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:24.240
to try to present him with stuff,

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:26.270
but you learn pretty early on that like

00:15:26.270 --> 00:15:28.050
there's always going to be what's next,

00:15:28.050 --> 00:15:30.340
which kind of made you comfortable with

00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:32.840
just kind of always being on your toes,

00:15:32.840 --> 00:15:35.580
always being ready to
adapt and do something new

00:15:35.580 --> 00:15:36.670
and to not get stagnant,

00:15:36.670 --> 00:15:39.060
and not get comfortable
doing the day to day stuff.

00:15:39.060 --> 00:15:40.550
- It's so obvious to me
what's going to happen

00:15:40.550 --> 00:15:41.840
with DailyVee on Snapchat,

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:43.070
like

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:44.510
our execution, these next 12 months,

00:15:44.510 --> 00:15:47.080
it's gonna be so fucking crazy.

00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:49.660
- I thought that DailyVee was
just going to be like a name,

00:15:49.660 --> 00:15:51.580
not like we're going to do it.

00:15:51.580 --> 00:15:53.100
It's going to be like a DailyVee,

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:54.320
just kinda like more frequent,

00:15:54.320 --> 00:15:56.150
like not necessarily a DailyVee.

00:15:56.150 --> 00:15:57.640
So when it was happening and going on,

00:15:57.640 --> 00:16:01.810
so Drock was pretty much
responsible for shooting it all

00:16:01.810 --> 00:16:03.530
and then turning it around.

00:16:03.530 --> 00:16:04.590
And then, so, you know,

00:16:04.590 --> 00:16:07.060
that responsibility kind
of shifted to me too.

00:16:07.060 --> 00:16:09.790
It's kinda Drock will shoot
it and I will edit them.

00:16:10.630 --> 00:16:14.010
And in beginning, it's like,
Oh, this is like a real thing.

00:16:14.010 --> 00:16:15.739
This ain't no joke.

00:16:15.739 --> 00:16:16.572
This doesn't,

00:16:16.572 --> 00:16:18.489
this isn't just something to do on a...

00:16:18.489 --> 00:16:20.810
A couple of hours because these DailyVees

00:16:20.810 --> 00:16:22.520
weren't two and three minutes long.

00:16:22.520 --> 00:16:23.353
- This

00:16:24.433 --> 00:16:27.768
This daily vlog has been such a big deal.

00:16:27.768 --> 00:16:29.890
- [Man behind camera]
Why did you say that?

00:16:31.090 --> 00:16:32.410
- Just put that part there.

00:16:32.410 --> 00:16:34.840
- Yeah, it was definitely
like a transition.

00:16:34.840 --> 00:16:37.560
I'm just like, Oh, like it
was a little jarring at first,

00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:38.850
but then just like anything else,

00:16:38.850 --> 00:16:40.340
you figure out the cadence and figure out

00:16:40.340 --> 00:16:41.173
how to make it work,

00:16:41.173 --> 00:16:42.800
and then you get into that flow

00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:43.830
and then you just get it done

00:16:43.830 --> 00:16:45.730
until he changes up the format again.

00:16:45.730 --> 00:16:47.360
And then you're like
doing it all over again.

00:16:47.360 --> 00:16:48.193
But it's just like,

00:16:48.193 --> 00:16:51.240
you kind of get comfortable
with the change.

00:16:51.240 --> 00:16:54.110
- [Katie] Being comfortable
with change is not always easy.

00:16:54.110 --> 00:16:55.630
Let's take it over to Tyler Babin

00:16:55.630 --> 00:16:58.600
and hear why he made the
change to leave VaynerMedia.

00:16:58.600 --> 00:16:59.570
- My name is Tyler Babin.

00:16:59.570 --> 00:17:01.140
I worked on team Gary

00:17:01.140 --> 00:17:03.240
for about three and a half years total.

00:17:03.240 --> 00:17:07.070
Started in may of 2016,

00:17:07.070 --> 00:17:09.710
was a big editor for just
Instagram and Facebook clips,

00:17:09.710 --> 00:17:12.600
and then Drock and I
ended up alternating days

00:17:12.600 --> 00:17:13.940
and we were both traveling

00:17:13.940 --> 00:17:15.650
all over the place, filming DailyVee.

00:17:15.650 --> 00:17:18.400
And then I left that
position about a year ago

00:17:18.400 --> 00:17:20.580
to kind of just go out
and do my own thing.

00:17:20.580 --> 00:17:21.413
- I'm gonna miss you, bro.

00:17:21.413 --> 00:17:23.053
- I'm gonna miss you too man, a lot.

00:17:23.053 --> 00:17:25.200
- It's super surreal.

00:17:25.200 --> 00:17:26.959
I'm really happy though for you man.

00:17:26.959 --> 00:17:27.792
- I appreciate it.

00:17:27.792 --> 00:17:28.900
- I really feel good about it.

00:17:28.900 --> 00:17:30.020
- Yeah, I do too.

00:17:30.020 --> 00:17:32.396
It feels like the right move right now.

00:17:32.396 --> 00:17:33.906
- I think that's the right sentence,

00:17:33.906 --> 00:17:34.739
the right move right now.

00:17:34.739 --> 00:17:38.210
- There was a video that
I made at one point,

00:17:38.210 --> 00:17:40.010
it's called "99% happy".

00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:42.450
And Gary had done a whole speech

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:45.310
where he said he was 99% happy in his life

00:17:45.310 --> 00:17:49.260
and 1% being unhappy made him
literally change everything.

00:17:49.260 --> 00:17:50.720
- On my 30th birthday I was driving

00:17:50.720 --> 00:17:52.820
from New York to Jersey to the store

00:17:52.820 --> 00:17:56.230
and realized I was only 99% happy.

00:17:56.230 --> 00:17:59.230
That 1% maybe changed my entire life.

00:17:59.230 --> 00:18:03.690
If you're not 100% pumped every second,

00:18:03.690 --> 00:18:06.950
if you actually are happy it's the weekend

00:18:06.950 --> 00:18:08.740
or are pumped to go on vacation,

00:18:08.740 --> 00:18:09.867
your shit is broken in.

00:18:09.867 --> 00:18:13.680
- And I felt myself creeping
way above 1% of my life

00:18:13.680 --> 00:18:15.047
being unhappy, and I
said, all right, cool,

00:18:15.047 --> 00:18:18.340
it's just like, it's time to
leave and do something else.

00:18:18.340 --> 00:18:20.280
And I knew that I had
built up enough equity

00:18:20.280 --> 00:18:22.470
and enough relationships that I wasn't...

00:18:22.470 --> 00:18:24.200
No matter what happened
like I didn't think

00:18:24.200 --> 00:18:25.580
I would ever leave on bad terms

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:27.730
and I don't think I'm
on bad terms with anyone

00:18:27.730 --> 00:18:28.610
in that building.

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:30.310
And so for me, being
able to like step away

00:18:30.310 --> 00:18:32.000
and just say like, you know what?

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:34.150
I need to like feed my soul for a while

00:18:34.150 --> 00:18:37.277
and do the things that are on
my mind is really important.

00:18:37.277 --> 00:18:40.990
And so I think that there's
never a wrong thing with

00:18:40.990 --> 00:18:44.170
realizing once you have the
thing you wanted so badly

00:18:44.170 --> 00:18:47.250
that you can change your mind
and go a different route.

00:18:47.250 --> 00:18:48.110
Nothing's perfect.

00:18:48.110 --> 00:18:50.740
You can always go
backwards and you can like,

00:18:50.740 --> 00:18:52.310
life is not a linear thing.

00:18:52.310 --> 00:18:54.780
You can move up and down and
like go different directions.

00:18:54.780 --> 00:18:57.120
And so just being willing
to embrace that I think

00:18:57.120 --> 00:18:59.960
is where you will find
like actual true happiness.

00:18:59.960 --> 00:19:01.150
- I always play this out.

00:19:01.150 --> 00:19:03.510
Let's say, when you go to heaven,

00:19:03.510 --> 00:19:05.360
they can show you what would have happened

00:19:05.360 --> 00:19:07.700
because that's the only
way you'd ever see it.

00:19:07.700 --> 00:19:09.150
Let's just say,

00:19:09.150 --> 00:19:10.610
what people don't understand is,

00:19:10.610 --> 00:19:12.450
Oh, Basha you made the wrong call.

00:19:12.450 --> 00:19:14.510
You should have went to grad school

00:19:14.510 --> 00:19:16.460
instead of that
opportunity or this company

00:19:16.460 --> 00:19:17.870
instead of that company.

00:19:17.870 --> 00:19:19.300
But you'll never believe it.

00:19:19.300 --> 00:19:20.680
If you would have went there,

00:19:20.680 --> 00:19:21.610
you would have excel...

00:19:21.610 --> 00:19:23.140
You should've went to VaynerMedia,

00:19:23.140 --> 00:19:25.150
not there, but it's terrible.

00:19:25.150 --> 00:19:27.620
You actually went to
Vayner and it was perfect.

00:19:27.620 --> 00:19:29.320
And you rose like a Phoenix

00:19:29.320 --> 00:19:30.930
and you were the head of all sound

00:19:30.930 --> 00:19:32.140
and everything went great,

00:19:32.140 --> 00:19:34.480
but then you had to go
to Virginia on business

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:36.710
and you got hit by a
car and you died at 31.

00:19:36.710 --> 00:19:40.410
So to like look at two
ideas and think you can

00:19:40.410 --> 00:19:43.290
practically make a good
decision is laughable,

00:19:43.290 --> 00:19:45.740
literally flip a coin, just
pick the one that feels better

00:19:45.740 --> 00:19:47.430
right that second,

00:19:47.430 --> 00:19:49.210
and make it about you,

00:19:49.210 --> 00:19:50.890
because usually what kids struggle with

00:19:50.890 --> 00:19:52.440
is the decision they want to do

00:19:53.490 --> 00:19:55.770
versus the decision that they
think they're supposed to do

00:19:55.770 --> 00:19:57.700
based on the other voices.

00:19:57.700 --> 00:20:00.240
- [Katie] Do you remember India
from the "AskGaryVee" show?

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:02.660
She's going to talk about
her relationship with Gary.

00:20:02.660 --> 00:20:04.890
So let's see her side of the story.

00:20:04.890 --> 00:20:05.723
- India.

00:20:07.450 --> 00:20:10.640
I think it's time to get into the...

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:11.700
- Show.

00:20:11.700 --> 00:20:12.533
- Show.

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:16.440
Let's get into

00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:17.997
- The show.
- The show.

00:20:19.480 --> 00:20:20.313
- Oh nice.

00:20:20.313 --> 00:20:22.470
India, wait a minute. How
do we not talk about this?

00:20:22.470 --> 00:20:24.617
Because we talked about it in real life.

00:20:24.617 --> 00:20:26.470
You know, Vayner nation,

00:20:26.470 --> 00:20:27.530
look at this,

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:28.590
I mean, India...

00:20:29.570 --> 00:20:32.927
Talk about kissing up to the (mumbles)

00:20:32.927 --> 00:20:35.940
(laughing)

00:20:35.940 --> 00:20:37.290
- My name is India.

00:20:37.290 --> 00:20:42.220
I worked at VaynerMedia
from 2012 until August,

00:20:42.220 --> 00:20:44.543
very recently, August, 2019.

00:20:45.680 --> 00:20:48.010
I started as a community manager in 2012

00:20:48.010 --> 00:20:50.140
and worked my way through
a bunch of different roles

00:20:50.140 --> 00:20:52.770
at the company but got to
work with Gary for a few years

00:20:52.770 --> 00:20:54.960
as his producer and copywriter.

00:20:54.960 --> 00:20:56.750
And then just before I left the company,

00:20:56.750 --> 00:21:00.370
I was working on Vayner
talent as a creative producer.

00:21:00.370 --> 00:21:02.080
One big thing that's very personal to me

00:21:02.080 --> 00:21:03.930
that I changed about
myself working for Gary

00:21:03.930 --> 00:21:06.620
was my confidence just in the workplace

00:21:06.620 --> 00:21:10.340
and being able to sit
in a room as a woman,

00:21:10.340 --> 00:21:13.060
working with a lot of
men and state my opinion

00:21:13.060 --> 00:21:16.190
and stated very strongly and be confident

00:21:16.190 --> 00:21:17.363
in my own abilities.

