00:30:08.340 --> 00:30:12.189
I just bought a honey farm in Pasa Robles,

00:30:12.189 --> 00:30:13.937
and we now sell honey

00:30:13.937 --> 00:30:15.860
and I just think 80%
of them be like, okay,

00:30:15.860 --> 00:30:17.160
like what, what am I doing?

00:30:17.160 --> 00:30:19.730
Like, yes, comma.

00:30:19.730 --> 00:30:22.203
So when you build culture
and you build trust,

00:30:22.203 --> 00:30:23.930
you can go fast.

00:30:23.930 --> 00:30:26.740
- And so when you put a plan in place,

00:30:26.740 --> 00:30:28.870
or if you're a leader and you
say we're going to do this,

00:30:28.870 --> 00:30:31.050
and everyone says, cool, I'm ready to go

00:30:31.050 --> 00:30:34.130
and then you realize that
that isn't the right thing,

00:30:34.130 --> 00:30:36.040
most people are probably scared

00:30:36.040 --> 00:30:37.210
that people are going to judge them

00:30:37.210 --> 00:30:38.880
as a flip flopping leader,

00:30:38.880 --> 00:30:41.810
if they believe that that
then is the right thing.

00:30:41.810 --> 00:30:44.140
- Be binary about your decisions.

00:30:44.140 --> 00:30:46.490
Let me tell you what I mean by that.

00:30:46.490 --> 00:30:49.810
Don't overthink jumping
into your first job

00:30:49.810 --> 00:30:52.640
and then quit it the next
day if it doesn't feel right.

00:30:52.640 --> 00:30:57.050
It's not a end all be all if
the first one's not right.

00:30:57.050 --> 00:30:59.853
That doesn't mean you can't
be thoughtful along the way,

00:31:01.230 --> 00:31:03.100
It doesn't mean that
you can make a decision,

00:31:03.100 --> 00:31:05.180
you're doing it and then a
couple of weeks didn't even know,

00:31:05.180 --> 00:31:07.020
three weeks earlier, it seems so, right,

00:31:07.020 --> 00:31:07.853
you're like, wait a minute,

00:31:07.853 --> 00:31:09.140
this feels like a waste of time.

00:31:09.140 --> 00:31:11.780
And then even if you declare
it to your whole world

00:31:11.780 --> 00:31:13.370
that you're doing this,

00:31:13.370 --> 00:31:15.853
people fear changing their
mind out of judgment.

00:31:16.770 --> 00:31:18.540
- And then you start to go down a path

00:31:18.540 --> 00:31:20.520
that just gets too caught up

00:31:20.520 --> 00:31:23.750
where you're too far away
from being able to say,

00:31:23.750 --> 00:31:25.160
actually, no, this is the right thing.

00:31:25.160 --> 00:31:27.920
I've been presented with
new information, new data,

00:31:27.920 --> 00:31:29.830
and I'm making this decision.

00:31:29.830 --> 00:31:33.130
And communication goes into a lot of that,

00:31:33.130 --> 00:31:36.530
but there's this kind of
it's happening right now.

00:31:36.530 --> 00:31:41.530
Like, are we supposed to
do what's best for us first

00:31:41.700 --> 00:31:44.990
or best for other people
even if it's at the detriment

00:31:44.990 --> 00:31:47.120
of our own self?

00:31:47.120 --> 00:31:48.480
- [Katie] Though it seems like the ability

00:31:48.480 --> 00:31:50.690
to change your mind as instilled at birth,

00:31:50.690 --> 00:31:52.270
This is something you need to work on.

00:31:52.270 --> 00:31:54.750
Let's hear from Gary's
brother AJ on his mindset

00:31:54.750 --> 00:31:56.080
at an early age.

00:31:56.080 --> 00:31:57.777
- I've actually really shifted.

00:31:57.777 --> 00:32:02.777
I think that I naturally growing
up was a bit more focused

00:32:03.760 --> 00:32:07.960
and put a lot more energy
and put a lot more stock

00:32:07.960 --> 00:32:10.350
into your original thought and opinion.

00:32:10.350 --> 00:32:12.870
And I thought that if
you change your mind,

00:32:12.870 --> 00:32:15.920
then it just validated that
your original opinion was wrong.

00:32:15.920 --> 00:32:19.160
And so because Gary is so
willing and able to change his

00:32:19.160 --> 00:32:20.520
opinion in mine,

00:32:20.520 --> 00:32:22.230
I think there was...

00:32:22.230 --> 00:32:23.920
Gary and I really don't
have a lot of friction.

00:32:23.920 --> 00:32:26.690
- AJ and I, because of our age difference

00:32:26.690 --> 00:32:29.760
have almost a brother father
kind of thing going on.

00:32:29.760 --> 00:32:31.850
That's very kind of unique and special.

00:32:31.850 --> 00:32:32.980
And we've been,

00:32:32.980 --> 00:32:36.190
we've met so many people that are 11 or 12

00:32:36.190 --> 00:32:39.960
or nine or 10 years apart
and don't have what we have.

00:32:39.960 --> 00:32:40.793
You know, it's tough.

00:32:40.793 --> 00:32:44.453
I mean, I was out of the house
when he was seven, right.

00:32:44.453 --> 00:32:46.880
And so it's just a very unique thing.

00:32:46.880 --> 00:32:49.280
And we have a very,
very, very special bond.

00:32:49.280 --> 00:32:51.720
- We've nearly never fist fight

00:32:51.720 --> 00:32:53.690
and have never fought over business.

00:32:53.690 --> 00:32:55.780
If we ever fight it's
over the silliest shit,

00:32:55.780 --> 00:32:58.880
it's stuff like board games
and pick up basketball, right.

00:32:58.880 --> 00:33:01.890
- I once punched my brother
AJ on a basketball court.

00:33:01.890 --> 00:33:03.670
And I've never really said it

00:33:03.670 --> 00:33:05.630
because I don't want to
admit it actually happened.

00:33:05.630 --> 00:33:08.090
- But when there was
friction in the early days

00:33:08.090 --> 00:33:10.160
was probably centered a little bit around

00:33:10.160 --> 00:33:11.550
the changing your mind concept.

00:33:11.550 --> 00:33:15.500
And I think what I struggled
with the most was how

00:33:16.960 --> 00:33:21.690
constant it was and how effortless it was.

00:33:21.690 --> 00:33:23.080
It kind of almost,

00:33:23.080 --> 00:33:25.820
I think what I struggled
with was it almost

00:33:25.820 --> 00:33:28.060
invalidated the original thought

00:33:28.060 --> 00:33:30.040
and made it really tough for me to accept

00:33:30.040 --> 00:33:32.600
and receive initial thoughts from Gary.

00:33:32.600 --> 00:33:33.433
He would say something and be like,

00:33:33.433 --> 00:33:35.420
well, you're just gonna change
your damn mind tomorrow.

00:33:35.420 --> 00:33:37.140
Like, why should I even receive this?

00:33:37.140 --> 00:33:39.090
Why should I take this seriously?

00:33:39.090 --> 00:33:43.040
But I think as I got more
exposure and more experience to

00:33:43.040 --> 00:33:45.223
business and life and people,

00:33:46.170 --> 00:33:48.110
I grew to appreciate it a lot more.

00:33:48.110 --> 00:33:52.560
And I felt like I needed a shift
and it wasn't necessarily a

00:33:52.560 --> 00:33:53.410
conscious effort.

00:33:53.410 --> 00:33:55.120
I didn't say I need to be
able to change my mind more.

00:33:55.120 --> 00:33:57.130
I need to be able to change my mind more.

00:33:57.130 --> 00:33:59.870
I think it just came from experience.

00:33:59.870 --> 00:34:02.450
And I think it speaks a
little bit to the idea of us

00:34:02.450 --> 00:34:04.420
starting businesses and things like that.

00:34:04.420 --> 00:34:05.900
And whether it's starting a business

00:34:05.900 --> 00:34:08.620
or starting an initiative
within a company,

00:34:08.620 --> 00:34:09.453
I think that

00:34:10.700 --> 00:34:14.840
ultimately if you fail or
if something is inaccurate,

00:34:14.840 --> 00:34:15.673
it's okay.

00:34:15.673 --> 00:34:16.506
As long as you adjust.

00:34:16.506 --> 00:34:18.610
And as long as you adjust
towards the positive.

00:34:18.610 --> 00:34:19.740
- This has been historic.

00:34:19.740 --> 00:34:21.070
Like factories,

00:34:21.070 --> 00:34:23.400
factories had to adjust to machines.

00:34:23.400 --> 00:34:26.600
Farmers had to adjust the tractors.

00:34:26.600 --> 00:34:29.110
Retail stores had to adjust to registers

00:34:29.110 --> 00:34:31.543
that were computers versus cash.

00:34:32.530 --> 00:34:33.950
This is not complicated.

00:34:33.950 --> 00:34:35.050
This is historic.

00:34:35.050 --> 00:34:36.380
Here's the punchline.

00:34:36.380 --> 00:34:38.623
The market is the market.

00:34:38.623 --> 00:34:40.330
What is the 54 year old going to do?

00:34:40.330 --> 00:34:41.170
Adjust

00:34:43.070 --> 00:34:44.180
or loose.

00:34:44.180 --> 00:34:45.460
- [Katie] Something Gary likes to say

00:34:45.460 --> 00:34:46.960
before giving advice is,

00:34:46.960 --> 00:34:48.340
I never think I'm right,

00:34:48.340 --> 00:34:51.190
because Gary knows he's just
stating one man's opinion.

00:34:51.190 --> 00:34:52.400
In this next chapter,

00:34:52.400 --> 00:34:54.440
we're going to dive into
different perspectives

00:34:54.440 --> 00:34:56.692
on the topic.
- Tell me how to save my life.

00:34:56.692 --> 00:34:57.525
Tell me--

00:34:57.525 --> 00:34:59.082
- I'm gonna tell you right now, Will.

00:34:59.082 --> 00:35:00.610
I'm gonna tell you right now my guy.

00:35:00.610 --> 00:35:02.110
So this is one of my partner, Ryan.

00:35:02.110 --> 00:35:02.943
- How you doing?

00:35:02.943 --> 00:35:03.776
- Pleasure.

00:35:03.776 --> 00:35:05.340
- Your ego is usually the driving force

00:35:05.340 --> 00:35:09.070
behind the deep desire to be right.

00:35:09.070 --> 00:35:10.540
I have to be right.

00:35:10.540 --> 00:35:14.100
And they say like, be
selective in your battles,

00:35:14.100 --> 00:35:17.020
but sometimes peace is
better than being right.

00:35:17.020 --> 00:35:21.260
So if you have the ability
to firmly believe something,

00:35:21.260 --> 00:35:24.750
and recognized when
you're wrong and admit it

00:35:24.750 --> 00:35:27.600
and implement change accordingly,

00:35:27.600 --> 00:35:29.810
it means you have macro strength,

00:35:29.810 --> 00:35:32.190
not micro stubbornness.

00:35:32.190 --> 00:35:33.023
So to say.

00:35:33.023 --> 00:35:33.970
- People overthink it.

00:35:33.970 --> 00:35:36.870
And they have ego that I'm
the only one that does it best

00:35:36.870 --> 00:35:38.920
and nobody's ever good enough.

00:35:38.920 --> 00:35:42.720
Go within humility, tell them the truth,

00:35:42.720 --> 00:35:44.640
and then if they don't
deliver on it, fire them.

00:35:44.640 --> 00:35:47.100
I've watched so many people
that are great at their craft

00:35:47.100 --> 00:35:49.690
never build a business because of ego,

00:35:49.690 --> 00:35:52.410
that they like feeling that
they're better at this thing

00:35:52.410 --> 00:35:55.033
than anybody than actually
scaling a business.

00:35:55.950 --> 00:35:57.840
- You know, it's what I did essentially.

00:35:57.840 --> 00:35:59.150
Like I was in finance.

00:35:59.150 --> 00:36:02.010
I worked for Goldman Sachs for seven years

00:36:02.010 --> 00:36:04.550
and then pivoted to be in media,

00:36:04.550 --> 00:36:07.993
but women's lifestyle
media as a young male.

00:36:09.180 --> 00:36:10.840
And I was laughed at.

00:36:10.840 --> 00:36:13.180
People definitely laughed
behind my back being like,

00:36:13.180 --> 00:36:14.373
what is this guy doing?

00:36:15.830 --> 00:36:16.663
But I knew that,

00:36:16.663 --> 00:36:20.830
I just knew that I would
not be happy being in the

00:36:22.270 --> 00:36:25.550
corporate America flywheel where it's not

00:36:25.550 --> 00:36:27.140
solely based on merit

00:36:28.160 --> 00:36:30.730
and I wanted to be part of something that

00:36:30.730 --> 00:36:33.600
what I put into it was
hopefully what I got out of it.

00:36:33.600 --> 00:36:36.350
Or at least I seal my own faith.

00:36:36.350 --> 00:36:37.780
- Winning is happy.

00:36:37.780 --> 00:36:39.040
Not making more money,

00:36:39.040 --> 00:36:40.940
cause you focused only
on the tech company.

00:36:40.940 --> 00:36:42.010
What are you trying to accomplish?

00:36:42.010 --> 00:36:42.843
And by the way,

00:36:43.800 --> 00:36:45.813
it's okay if that shit changes.

00:36:47.690 --> 00:36:49.860
At 27, you might need money

00:36:50.762 --> 00:36:52.100
or a boyfriend or a girlfriend.

00:36:52.100 --> 00:36:55.930
At 34, you might want to
have more work life balance,

00:36:55.930 --> 00:36:57.330
cause you have a child now.

00:36:57.330 --> 00:36:58.483
At 41, you might need more money

00:36:58.483 --> 00:37:00.277
because you wasted your
money after you had a good...

00:37:00.277 --> 00:37:02.960
But like it's okay for it to change daily.

00:37:02.960 --> 00:37:06.280
- For me, when moving
from finance to media,

00:37:06.280 --> 00:37:09.450
I convinced myself
whether it was true or not

00:37:09.450 --> 00:37:14.090
that if I failed that I can
easily go back to finance

00:37:14.090 --> 00:37:17.220
even if I took one step back,
I can go back to finance,

00:37:17.220 --> 00:37:18.533
get a job, get a salary.

00:37:19.500 --> 00:37:22.800
So I think that you need to
think worst case scenario

00:37:22.800 --> 00:37:24.390
and are you okay financially?

00:37:24.390 --> 00:37:28.070
Are you okay with the lifestyle
you might have to lead

00:37:28.070 --> 00:37:29.870
if you do fail entirely?

00:37:29.870 --> 00:37:31.270
Do you have a family?

00:37:31.270 --> 00:37:32.720
Do you have kids?

00:37:32.720 --> 00:37:34.420
Are you single?

00:37:34.420 --> 00:37:36.870
Do you have dependence
that you need to make

00:37:36.870 --> 00:37:39.623
a minimum amount of money in
order to support those people?

00:37:39.623 --> 00:37:42.810
Because that might be
a driving force behind

00:37:42.810 --> 00:37:44.010
taking a certain risk.

00:37:44.010 --> 00:37:46.850
So I don't think you should...

00:37:46.850 --> 00:37:47.683
I don't think...

00:37:47.683 --> 00:37:49.540
I'm not saying that you should do that.

00:37:49.540 --> 00:37:51.540
It create fear in your mind,

00:37:51.540 --> 00:37:54.040
but you need to understand
what your downside is,

00:37:54.040 --> 00:37:55.770
what your worst case scenario is.

00:37:55.770 --> 00:37:57.950
And that will help give you comfort

00:37:57.950 --> 00:37:59.400
because if you can convince yourself

00:37:59.400 --> 00:38:01.600
that you're okay with that downside,

00:38:01.600 --> 00:38:04.810
you're going to go all in and
you're not going to look back.

00:38:04.810 --> 00:38:05.877
- [Katie] Gary likes to talk about

00:38:05.877 --> 00:38:07.920
how he missed out on investing in Uber

00:38:07.920 --> 00:38:09.410
during the angel round.

00:38:09.410 --> 00:38:11.920
What we haven't heard is
AJ's side of the story.

00:38:11.920 --> 00:38:13.770
So let's take a look.

00:38:13.770 --> 00:38:17.040
- You know, I guess this is a fun example.

00:38:17.040 --> 00:38:18.590
Gary and I had the opportunity to invest

00:38:18.590 --> 00:38:20.930
in the seed round in Uber.

00:38:20.930 --> 00:38:22.220
Gary talks about it all the time.

00:38:22.220 --> 00:38:25.410
- My most devastating business decisions

00:38:25.410 --> 00:38:27.610
have been predicated at the moments

00:38:27.610 --> 00:38:29.193
when I bought my two homes.

00:38:30.070 --> 00:38:30.903
I passed,

00:38:30.903 --> 00:38:32.930
I've never said this clearly,

00:38:32.930 --> 00:38:35.950
I passed on Uber because
I bought an apartment.

00:38:35.950 --> 00:38:37.840
If I had just stayed in
the apartment I was in

00:38:37.840 --> 00:38:40.573
for one more year, I
would have $400 million.

00:38:42.300 --> 00:38:43.510
That I don't have right now.

00:38:43.510 --> 00:38:45.530
That's about as basic as I can say it.

00:38:45.530 --> 00:38:47.940
- And then they re approached
us for the series B.

00:38:47.940 --> 00:38:48.810
And they're like, come on guys,

00:38:48.810 --> 00:38:50.640
cause these are personal friends,

00:38:50.640 --> 00:38:51.590
like, come on guys,

00:38:52.450 --> 00:38:53.727
I know you miss out on the seed,

00:38:53.727 --> 00:38:55.820
but it's been a little while
we have really good tracks

00:38:55.820 --> 00:38:57.310
and we'd love to have you for the ride.

00:38:57.310 --> 00:38:58.143
Right?

00:38:58.143 --> 00:38:59.860
And I think somebody that
maybe would be too stuck

00:38:59.860 --> 00:39:04.860
in the mud would cut their
nose to spite their face,

00:39:05.520 --> 00:39:06.380
and not invest.

00:39:06.380 --> 00:39:08.410
But you know, Gary and
I said, you know what?

00:39:08.410 --> 00:39:09.560
Let's keep our nose in tack,

00:39:09.560 --> 00:39:10.520
let's bite the bullet.

00:39:10.520 --> 00:39:12.160
Let's forget about our regret.

00:39:12.160 --> 00:39:12.993
Let's move forward.

00:39:12.993 --> 00:39:14.350
We don't wanna regret twice,

00:39:15.230 --> 00:39:16.380
it's better late than never.

00:39:16.380 --> 00:39:18.310
And so we ended up investing
in Uber in a series B

00:39:18.310 --> 00:39:19.830
and did really well because of it.

00:39:19.830 --> 00:39:21.177
Could have done better at the seed,

00:39:21.177 --> 00:39:23.760
but that's life, you take
your losses along the way.

00:39:23.760 --> 00:39:25.510
The single biggest reason,

00:39:25.510 --> 00:39:27.883
Vayner X got to where it got to,

00:39:29.080 --> 00:39:30.830
beyond Gary's brand equity

00:39:30.830 --> 00:39:33.890
and a salesmanship and
his and his public brand

00:39:33.890 --> 00:39:38.510
is our disproportionate willingness
to communicate top-down.

00:39:38.510 --> 00:39:39.930
And so,

00:39:39.930 --> 00:39:43.320
spending the time and energy
to talk to your executive team,

00:39:43.320 --> 00:39:45.640
as well as your entry level employee,

00:39:45.640 --> 00:39:47.250
that just started two weeks ago.

00:39:47.250 --> 00:39:48.170
- It's about people,

00:39:48.170 --> 00:39:49.820
when you go from one to five locations,

00:39:49.820 --> 00:39:50.653
it's about the people.

00:39:50.653 --> 00:39:53.876
- And I think just having an
authentic line of communication

00:39:53.876 --> 00:39:56.490
and an open door policy
that people actually

00:39:56.490 --> 00:39:59.790
believe to be true is massively important

00:39:59.790 --> 00:40:02.220
when shifting through change.

00:40:02.220 --> 00:40:03.300
- [Katie] Being the bigger person

00:40:03.300 --> 00:40:05.630
is not always the easiest thing to do.

00:40:05.630 --> 00:40:07.850
Let's hear from Marc
Yudkin on how Gary shaped

00:40:07.850 --> 00:40:09.330
his perspective on this.

00:40:09.330 --> 00:40:10.300
- I'm Marc Yudkin.

00:40:10.300 --> 00:40:12.250
I'm our chief operating officer

00:40:12.250 --> 00:40:15.560
and general counsel of all of Vayner X.

00:40:15.560 --> 00:40:20.560
I've known Gary for
about 13 to 14 years now.

00:40:20.940 --> 00:40:24.110
I did a lot of his legal
work as outside counsel,

00:40:24.110 --> 00:40:27.210
before VaynerMedia was formed.

00:40:27.210 --> 00:40:30.110
I did his investment
work in Uber and Tumblr

00:40:30.110 --> 00:40:32.070
and Twitter and Facebook
and all that stuff.

00:40:32.070 --> 00:40:34.720
So I was the lawyer for those deals.

00:40:34.720 --> 00:40:37.460
So I've gotten pretty
good insight into Gary

00:40:37.460 --> 00:40:41.420
for the last 13 or 14 years
and it's been a fun ride.

00:40:41.420 --> 00:40:43.610
I tend to be, I tend to
think I'm usually right,

00:40:43.610 --> 00:40:45.070
because I usually am,

00:40:45.070 --> 00:40:49.760
but it's been harder for
me to sometimes bite my lip

00:40:49.760 --> 00:40:51.780
and kind of be the
bigger man and walk away,

00:40:51.780 --> 00:40:54.080
even though I know it's
the right thing to do.

00:40:54.920 --> 00:40:58.580
And I think he has taught
me that sometimes there's

00:40:58.580 --> 00:41:02.190
a greater benefit to even
though you might be right

00:41:02.190 --> 00:41:04.680
to just kind of walk away
and be the bigger man

00:41:04.680 --> 00:41:07.830
in a situation cause the
benefits derived later on

00:41:07.830 --> 00:41:10.330
will far out seed the

00:41:12.140 --> 00:41:13.460
advantages now of saying,

00:41:13.460 --> 00:41:14.380
Hey, I told you so

00:41:14.380 --> 00:41:16.690
or Hey, I'm right now
in this particular case.

00:41:16.690 --> 00:41:18.600
So it's not a particular instance,

00:41:18.600 --> 00:41:21.810
but I definitely think it's
a particular character trait

00:41:21.810 --> 00:41:23.313
it was tough for me at first.

00:41:24.170 --> 00:41:26.020
But to realize that in the end,

00:41:26.020 --> 00:41:28.830
it's always better to
be the bigger person.

00:41:28.830 --> 00:41:30.700
- It's one of the things that
I'm spending a lot of time on

00:41:30.700 --> 00:41:33.700
one that a lot of people
aren't capable of executing,

00:41:33.700 --> 00:41:36.260
which is how much better life
is when you're the bigger man

00:41:36.260 --> 00:41:38.240
and woman in every situation,

00:41:38.240 --> 00:41:41.720
no matter if you're right or
wrong, no matter what it is.

00:41:41.720 --> 00:41:44.340
Are you capable of swallowing your pride

00:41:44.340 --> 00:41:48.450
and having the humility
to say you're sorry

00:41:48.450 --> 00:41:53.090
even when maybe it wasn't
yours to say you're sorry?

00:41:53.090 --> 00:41:58.090
Leadership and happiness
really really really comes

00:41:58.780 --> 00:42:01.013
at the ability to,

00:42:03.220 --> 00:42:06.040
to focus on one move, which is,

00:42:06.040 --> 00:42:07.210
can you be the bigger person?

00:42:07.210 --> 00:42:09.030
For example, being a good
number two, three, four

00:42:09.030 --> 00:42:10.490
at VaynerMedia is pretty simple.

00:42:10.490 --> 00:42:12.840
Like I'm going to care if
you're capable of being

00:42:12.840 --> 00:42:13.940
the bigger person.

00:42:13.940 --> 00:42:14.773
Empathy.

00:42:14.773 --> 00:42:17.020
A leader that leads positive energy.

00:42:17.020 --> 00:42:18.140
Like you're not...

00:42:18.140 --> 00:42:21.150
Like you can sell trillion
dollars in my company,

00:42:21.150 --> 00:42:22.800
if you're a dick face
you're going to lose.

00:42:22.800 --> 00:42:24.660
- Changing your mind shows growth,

00:42:24.660 --> 00:42:27.140
changing your mind shows vulnerability

00:42:27.140 --> 00:42:29.560
and vulnerability is one of
the strongest things that

00:42:29.560 --> 00:42:30.710
you could have these days.

00:42:30.710 --> 00:42:33.680
I mean, everybody is so
guarded and so defensive

00:42:33.680 --> 00:42:35.430
and you know, I think right now,

00:42:35.430 --> 00:42:38.670
especially we're seeing a huge
moment where a lot of people

00:42:38.670 --> 00:42:40.680
are being asked to change their mind,

00:42:40.680 --> 00:42:42.680
and you could say that it's

00:42:42.680 --> 00:42:45.130
one political extremist and
you're trying to get them all

00:42:45.130 --> 00:42:46.960
the way to the other extreme,
but it's not, you know,

00:42:46.960 --> 00:42:49.300
it's like people within
even a small faction,

00:42:49.300 --> 00:42:51.590
just asking for a little mind shift.

00:42:51.590 --> 00:42:53.490
And I think that

00:42:53.490 --> 00:42:58.490
that shows tremendous growth
and an ability to really become

00:42:58.610 --> 00:43:02.160
malleable and allow yourself
to be shaped by experiences

00:43:02.160 --> 00:43:04.380
because we are shaped by our experiences.

00:43:04.380 --> 00:43:06.690
The person you are today is shaped by

00:43:06.690 --> 00:43:07.620
what you experienced before.

00:43:07.620 --> 00:43:08.770
So why shouldn't you change?

00:43:08.770 --> 00:43:10.070
Your life is not static.

00:43:10.070 --> 00:43:12.580
Your life is never going to be
the same thing over and over.

