We have to normalize,

00:54:33.950 --> 00:54:36.450
changing your mind is a strength

00:54:36.450 --> 00:54:38.070
because we get new information

00:54:38.070 --> 00:54:41.750
and we live in a 24 hour
real time environment

00:54:41.750 --> 00:54:43.490
where if you get new information

00:54:43.490 --> 00:54:45.120
and you don't change your mind,

00:54:45.120 --> 00:54:46.420
then you're being...

00:54:46.420 --> 00:54:49.110
You're disservice to yourself
and everyone around you.

00:54:49.110 --> 00:54:51.030
Because I always say like,

00:54:51.030 --> 00:54:52.810
if I'm not learning, I'm not growing,

00:54:52.810 --> 00:54:54.050
if I'm not growing, I'm dying.

00:54:54.050 --> 00:54:57.070
So like, changing your
mind and being comfortable

00:54:57.070 --> 00:54:58.410
with changing your mind

00:54:58.410 --> 00:55:00.500
means that you're growing
because you're taking in

00:55:00.500 --> 00:55:02.750
the new information that
you have been exposed to

00:55:02.750 --> 00:55:05.170
and you're actually using
it to do something better.

00:55:05.170 --> 00:55:06.470
- I think the important thing is,

00:55:06.470 --> 00:55:09.470
and particularly now in
the face of a pandemic

00:55:09.470 --> 00:55:12.040
and in the face of a world
that continues to change

00:55:12.040 --> 00:55:14.010
and acknowledge things like,

00:55:14.010 --> 00:55:16.800
racial injustice and social injustice,

00:55:16.800 --> 00:55:20.920
we're realizing that we as
leaders need to be listening

00:55:20.920 --> 00:55:23.830
and learning and pivoting all the time.

00:55:23.830 --> 00:55:26.140
And where we'll fail, frankly,

00:55:26.140 --> 00:55:28.400
is when we think we know the answer

00:55:28.400 --> 00:55:31.130
and that we just keep
blindly going down a path

00:55:31.130 --> 00:55:33.620
without always checking ourselves.

00:55:33.620 --> 00:55:36.180
And I think now more than ever,

00:55:36.180 --> 00:55:38.070
we're in this very...

00:55:38.070 --> 00:55:40.480
We're in a tougher place
now than we were even

00:55:40.480 --> 00:55:42.090
at the beginning of COVID.

00:55:42.090 --> 00:55:43.400
We need to be more sensitive,

00:55:43.400 --> 00:55:45.820
We need to learn, we need to take action,

00:55:45.820 --> 00:55:47.990
but it's gotta be the right action.

00:55:47.990 --> 00:55:51.620
And so to me, the, the whole
idea of changing your mind,

00:55:51.620 --> 00:55:53.900
it sounds like it's a
negative in our society

00:55:53.900 --> 00:55:54.733
for some reason,

00:55:54.733 --> 00:55:58.130
it sounds like you're ambivalent
or that you're disingenuous

00:55:58.130 --> 00:55:59.900
or that you're,

00:55:59.900 --> 00:56:02.440
you're not committed or
you're not intelligent,

00:56:02.440 --> 00:56:04.150
but it actually is a strength.

00:56:04.150 --> 00:56:06.590
And it's something that
I think if more leaders

00:56:06.590 --> 00:56:08.940
could subscribed to celebrating that,

00:56:08.940 --> 00:56:11.120
and maybe there's a different
way of even framing up

00:56:11.120 --> 00:56:15.140
that narrative, I think we'd
be in a much better place.

00:56:15.140 --> 00:56:16.150
- You know, it's really funny.

00:56:16.150 --> 00:56:17.490
I always talk about,
don't be in the middle,

00:56:17.490 --> 00:56:18.510
don't be half pregnant,

00:56:18.510 --> 00:56:19.830
I pull from opposite directions,

00:56:19.830 --> 00:56:20.706
I have...

00:56:20.706 --> 00:56:23.200
A lot of what is interesting about me is

00:56:23.200 --> 00:56:26.480
I see very very different people
associated with my message.

00:56:26.480 --> 00:56:28.920
People that genuinely don't
agree on a single thing

00:56:28.920 --> 00:56:31.640
politically or socially
because they're hearing

00:56:31.640 --> 00:56:34.230
different things from
me and I bounce around

00:56:34.230 --> 00:56:36.143
and I'm been thinking a lot about it,

00:56:37.360 --> 00:56:40.410
parenting philosophy,
entrepreneurship philosophy,

00:56:40.410 --> 00:56:41.900
obviously political philosophy,

00:56:41.900 --> 00:56:44.090
like we are pulling
further and further apart

00:56:44.090 --> 00:56:44.923
from each other.

00:56:44.923 --> 00:56:47.340
People are pulling in
such opposite directions,

00:56:47.340 --> 00:56:51.060
yet I feel like there's so many merits

00:56:51.060 --> 00:56:53.390
and so many different
nuanced points of view

00:56:53.390 --> 00:56:56.330
and I just think that we've
become so one dimensional

00:56:56.330 --> 00:56:58.140
and so absolute.

00:56:58.140 --> 00:57:00.830
I think we have to start talking
more about allowing people

00:57:00.830 --> 00:57:02.610
to change their mind.

00:57:02.610 --> 00:57:05.330
I think if we make changing your mind,

00:57:05.330 --> 00:57:09.193
something that is put on a
pedestal instead of demonized,

00:57:10.070 --> 00:57:11.970
I think that would really help us.

00:57:11.970 --> 00:57:13.750
- Gary doesn't believe in

00:57:15.220 --> 00:57:18.460
one subjective opinion,

00:57:18.460 --> 00:57:22.610
being the person that can
say that idea is the one that

00:57:22.610 --> 00:57:24.250
should be executed,

00:57:24.250 --> 00:57:27.890
especially if it has minimal
financial risk to it.

00:57:27.890 --> 00:57:31.083
It's like do them all and
see what the consumer thinks.

00:57:32.050 --> 00:57:34.543
Find right instead of guess right.

00:57:35.460 --> 00:57:39.910
And our industry as a whole
is much more guess right

00:57:39.910 --> 00:57:41.600
versus find right because

00:57:42.440 --> 00:57:44.100
they'll say that it's schizophrenia,

00:57:44.100 --> 00:57:46.650
or they'll say that it's
throwing things against the wall

00:57:46.650 --> 00:57:50.570
until it sticks or it's not
on brand or whatever it may be

00:57:50.570 --> 00:57:54.710
where we don't necessarily
believe in that as much

00:57:54.710 --> 00:57:58.260
because we think that the
consumer should decide

00:57:58.260 --> 00:58:01.700
what is quality versus
three people in a board room

00:58:01.700 --> 00:58:04.430
deciding what is quality based on

00:58:04.430 --> 00:58:07.980
what their opinion of quality is.

00:58:07.980 --> 00:58:11.100
- And I think a lot about
that now with my content,

00:58:11.100 --> 00:58:13.410
which is why am I so
comfortable with trying things?

00:58:13.410 --> 00:58:15.730
I posted a video yesterday
that was upside down

00:58:15.730 --> 00:58:16.563
on Instagram.

00:58:16.563 --> 00:58:18.350
I don't know how many of you saw it.

00:58:20.873 --> 00:58:22.860
And then a couple of days earlier,

00:58:22.860 --> 00:58:24.900
I made a video where I
just said, like this post,

00:58:24.900 --> 00:58:27.350
like 58 times in a row,

00:58:27.350 --> 00:58:30.910
and I've been using this hash
tag in the two posts that,

00:58:30.910 --> 00:58:32.290
always be testing.

00:58:32.290 --> 00:58:34.840
And it's really my way to give you a nod

00:58:34.840 --> 00:58:36.210
of giving you permission.

00:58:36.210 --> 00:58:39.000
The like like like video did, okay.

00:58:39.000 --> 00:58:41.120
The upside down video
yesterday did not do well.

00:58:41.120 --> 00:58:42.350
And that excites me.

00:58:42.350 --> 00:58:46.430
And I'm excited to spend more
time over the next year or two

00:58:46.430 --> 00:58:48.860
of trying things completely left field

00:58:48.860 --> 00:58:51.420
to hopefully inspire people to,

00:58:51.420 --> 00:58:54.060
what I'm so unbelievably concerned about

00:58:54.060 --> 00:58:55.990
for everybody in here
who's creating content,

00:58:55.990 --> 00:58:58.200
whether on YouTube or
Instagram or other places

00:58:58.200 --> 00:59:02.090
is you've become so
obsessed with the numbers

00:59:02.970 --> 00:59:04.590
associated with the posts

00:59:04.590 --> 00:59:06.490
that you're not actually
even putting out any more

00:59:06.490 --> 00:59:07.750
what you want to put out,

00:59:07.750 --> 00:59:10.380
you're putting out what
you know is going to work.

00:59:10.380 --> 00:59:12.010
Too many of you are not doing things

00:59:12.010 --> 00:59:13.830
because you're worried about
how it's going to make you look

00:59:13.830 --> 00:59:14.980
cause you're late,

00:59:14.980 --> 00:59:19.980
so you're not starting, which
is always a bad idea, right?

00:59:20.200 --> 00:59:21.500
It's a huge thing happening.

00:59:21.500 --> 00:59:22.360
I think this...

00:59:22.360 --> 00:59:24.360
I'm always thinking about the
sound bite that may help you.

00:59:24.360 --> 00:59:25.193
I'm pumped right now

00:59:25.193 --> 00:59:26.620
cause I have a funny feeling
two or three were like,

00:59:26.620 --> 00:59:28.690
fuck, maybe I should...

00:59:28.690 --> 00:59:29.523
I get it.

00:59:29.523 --> 00:59:30.910
I understand brand.

00:59:30.910 --> 00:59:32.080
I understand shit.

00:59:32.080 --> 00:59:32.990
I'm supposed to be this.

00:59:32.990 --> 00:59:34.600
And I have less than all these,

00:59:34.600 --> 00:59:36.040
but if you don't start, you don't get.

00:59:36.040 --> 00:59:39.260
And by the way, cream
always rises to the top.

00:59:39.260 --> 00:59:41.610
If you're so good, right?

00:59:41.610 --> 00:59:42.720
You'll be there.

00:59:42.720 --> 00:59:44.460
- [Katie] Well guys,
you've made it this far.

00:59:44.460 --> 00:59:46.160
Thanks for watching until the end.

00:59:46.160 --> 00:59:47.387
Now we'll take it over to Gary.

00:59:47.387 --> 00:59:50.110
- The thing that baffles my mind is that

00:59:50.110 --> 00:59:52.120
people don't realize that

00:59:52.120 --> 00:59:55.680
there is no Monday morning
quarterbacking in life.

00:59:55.680 --> 00:59:57.130
You can talk on Monday morning

00:59:57.130 --> 00:59:59.540
and be upset when your quarterback did.

00:59:59.540 --> 01:00:00.950
Sam Darnold, I need you this year.

01:00:00.950 --> 01:00:02.800
But in real life,

01:00:02.800 --> 01:00:06.500
when you decide to buy
real estate in the Hamptons

01:00:06.500 --> 01:00:07.333
and it doesn't work out

01:00:07.333 --> 01:00:10.180
or buy a $5 sneaker and become a flipper

01:00:10.180 --> 01:00:11.530
instead of a lawyer,

01:00:11.530 --> 01:00:13.720
you don't know how your
life plays out as a lawyer.

01:00:13.720 --> 01:00:16.950
So you're either an optimistic
or pessimistic person.

01:00:16.950 --> 01:00:19.430
The optimistic person
goes and does flip life.

01:00:19.430 --> 01:00:20.450
It doesn't work out.

01:00:20.450 --> 01:00:22.730
And they realize, well, I
would have been the law thing.

01:00:22.730 --> 01:00:23.610
I would have made some more money,

01:00:23.610 --> 01:00:24.780
but I would have been so unhappy.

01:00:24.780 --> 01:00:27.160
And I'm glad because now I'm this thing.

01:00:27.160 --> 01:00:29.500
I'm a teacher in this high
school and I'm super happy.

01:00:29.500 --> 01:00:31.860
The pessimistic person
does great in flipping

01:00:31.860 --> 01:00:33.320
does a million a year and decides

01:00:33.320 --> 01:00:35.900
they're going to be 3 million
a year as being a lawyer

01:00:35.900 --> 01:00:37.650
and that's the only thing
they value is money.

01:00:37.650 --> 01:00:39.900
So they fucking lost.

01:00:39.900 --> 01:00:41.010
You decide,

01:00:41.010 --> 01:00:42.870
you decide if you're winning and losing

01:00:42.870 --> 01:00:44.210
before it even happens.

01:00:44.210 --> 01:00:46.980
So like, can we just once and for all,

01:00:46.980 --> 01:00:50.410
make people understand
that they have no idea

01:00:50.410 --> 01:00:52.530
that knowing what the right decision is

01:00:52.530 --> 01:00:54.360
at the time you're making the decision

01:00:54.360 --> 01:00:56.166
is humanly impossible.

01:00:56.166 --> 01:00:58.833
(upbeat music)

